Based on review of `models/database.py`, `tests/conftest.py`, `main.py`, and `models/__init__.py`:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "tests/conftest.py",
    "line": "48-50 (duplicates models/database.py:44-47)",
    "summary": "The `client` fixture's inner `get_test_session()` re-implements `get_session()`'s body (`with Session(engine) as session: yield session`) verbatim instead of parameterizing the real `get_session` by engine.",
    "failure_scenario": "If get_session's session handling ever changes (e.g. adding rollback-on-exception, a commit policy, or switching to session.begin()), only models/database.py gets updated — the copy in conftest.py silently keeps the old behavior, so the test suite exercises different session-lifecycle semantics than production and a real session-handling bug can pass all tests. Simpler form: make get_session take the engine as a parameter (default to the module-level `engine`) or expose a small `make_session_dependency(engine) -> Callable`, so conftest.py reuses the one implementation with a different engine instead of retyping the with-block."
  },
  {
    "file": "models/database.py",
    "line": "18-21",
    "summary": "`get_database_url()` reads `MISSION_CONTROL_DATABASE_URL` from the environment, but nothing in the diff ever sets that variable, and tests bypass it entirely — conftest.py calls `make_engine(f\"sqlite:///{db_path}\")` with an explicit URL, not through this function.",
    "failure_scenario": "The docstring claims 'tests use this to point at a real temporary SQLite file instead of the dev DB,' which is false — a maintainer who trusts it and sets the env var expecting the test suite to honor it will be confused when tests ignore it (only the never-overridden default `engine` is affected). It's speculative configurability with no current caller. Simpler form: drop get_database_url and inline `DEFAULT_DATABASE_URL` as make_engine's own default (`database_url: str = DEFAULT_DATABASE_URL`), or at minimum fix the misleading docstring, until an actual caller needs env-based configuration."
  },
  {
    "file": "main.py",
    "line": "15-29",
    "summary": "`create_app()` calls `create_db_and_tables()` unconditionally at import time against the default dev engine, and this runs every time `from main import app` executes — including inside tests/conftest.py's `client` fixture, which separately creates tables on its own temp engine.",
    "failure_scenario": "Running the test suite touches a stray ./mission_control.db file on disk as an import side effect that has nothing to do with what's being tested (shows up in `git status`/CI workspaces, confusing to trace back). If create_db_and_tables later becomes non-idempotent or slow (a real migration step), it silently taxes every test run through an import nobody expected to hit disk. Simpler form: move the table-creation call out of create_app() into an explicit startup path (FastAPI startup event, or just the documented seed/run script) so importing `main.app` has no disk side effects."
  },
  {
    "file": "models/__init__.py",
    "line": "14-40",
    "summary": "Entity re-exports are maintained as two separate hand-written lists that must stay in sync by name: the import block (14-24) and `__all__` (26-40).",
    "failure_scenario": "Adding the next entity module (T2+, expected per the file's own docstring) requires updating both lists; forgetting the `__all__` entry doesn't error — it just silently omits the new model from `from models import Foo`/`import *`, a mistake that surfaces later as a confusing ImportError far from the actual omission. Simpler form: drop the hand-maintained `__all__` and rely on the explicit per-module imports call sites already use (`from models.mission import Mission`), removing the second list this scaffold doesn't otherwise need."
  }
]
```
